<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Navajo Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – present · capital Window Rock</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest Native American reservation and self-governing nation in the United States, founded on the treaty of 1868 that ended the Navajo exile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1863 and 1864 the US Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kit-carson">Kit Carson</a> waged a scorched-earth campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Diné</a>, then forced about 9,000 of them on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-walk-of-the-navajo">Long Walk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo">Bosque Redondo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, where many died. The Treaty of 1868, signed on 1 June, allowed the Navajo to return to a reservation within their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> between four sacred mountains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The reservation was repeatedly enlarged and now covers about 70,000 square kilometres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>. A tribal council was created in 1923, chiefly to approve oil leases, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-chee-dodge">Henry Chee Dodge</a> as chairman, and Window Rock became the capital in the 1930s. The federal livestock reduction of the 1930s devastated herding families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo-code-talkers">Navajo Code Talkers</a> served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">Marines</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The tribe adopted the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> Nation in 1969 and reorganised its government into three branches from 1989. It continues as a sovereign nation within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, contending with poverty and the legacy of uranium mining while maintaining one of the most widely spoken Native languages.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/navajo-nation.jpg" alt="Navajo Nation" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Jay R. Degroat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_design_of_the_Navajo_Nation_flag_as_it_was_adopted_on_May_21,_1968.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1868 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Window Rock</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry Chee Dodge, Peter MacDonald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Navajo religion, Native American Church, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Navajo, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dine, Naabeeho Binahasdzo</dd></dl></aside></div>

